joystick junkies playing too many video  games is an illness it's what parents of  fortnite fanatics have been saying all  along  these are just a handful of the  headlines from 2018 that followed the  news that the world health organization  had defined gaming disorder in its  international classification of diseases  from fortnight to candy crush concerns  have long been raised about game's  addictive potential but for it to be  classified as a distinct disorder was a  big step however the psychology  community found itself divided some  psychologists believe the who's decision  was based on poorly constructed research  and could even spark a moral panic we're  not  that far advanced in our understanding  of something that you might want to call  video game addiction now compared to 20  years ago really meanwhile here in the  uk psychologists at a specialist run  clinic have reported success at treating  the condition we've been quite surprised  how severe it can be for those that are  actually affected so was the who right  to make this decision and does gaming  addiction really exist  in 2018 gaming disorder was outlined in  the icd-11 the latest version of a  manual which outlines health conditions  around the world the diagnostic criteria  for gaming disorder essentially boils  down to these factors persisting over a  period of at least 12 months also  included are boundaries of normality  these ensure that normal behaviors like  using games to alleviate boredom or  playing games to socialize with friends  are no indication anything is wrong  those boundaries of normality are  important because they outline the  difference between a normal healthy  relationship to gaming and an unhealthy  harmful one which helps limit the  potential for misdiagnosis by worried  parents who might not know all that much  about gaming it's also useful for the  mainstream press as it helps ensure the  condition is reported with the level of  nuance and detail that such a serious  subject matter calls for  yeah about that the who's announcement  led to a predictable response with the  sort of panic inducing headlines we saw  earlier phrases like kids addicted to  video games like fortnite will now have  the condition recognized as an official  mental disorder seen tailor-made to make  parents rip the controllers from their  kids hands and throw their playstations  out the window by the way it's  interesting that they keep mentioning  fortnite but we'll come back to that  this is pete echels he's a professor of  psychology and science communication at  bath spa university and the author of  lost in a good game a book about the  psychological effects good and bad of  video games if you look at worries about  obsessive play with video games they've  been around since the early 1980s bunch  of doctors who had these three guys that  came into their clinic that all  presented with very similar symptoms all  three of them were on the verge of  getting married all three of them  developed quite  strong obsessions with playing space  invaders so they take this sort of weird  psychoanalytic freudian approach to it  and say well the reason that they became  obsessed with space invaders is because  they saw themselves as the tank  and their prospective brides to be with  a invading alien force coming into their  homes which is a really depressing way  of thinking about marriage it was the  start in a way of this  worry around you know you look at  somebody playing video game and they're  really engrossed in it they play it for  a long time this maybe doesn't seem  right we're not  that far advanced in our understanding  of something that you might want to call  video game addiction now compared to 20  years ago really in the wake of the  who's decision pete was one of a number  of psychologists around the world who  voiced his concerns co-authoring a  comment paper that called for the who to  er on the side of caution this paper was  one of dozens published by psychologists  many of whom took issue with the ways  much of the research into gaming  disorder had been conducted by adapting  questionnaires designed to measure other  addictions we know that there are other  things that are addictive so let's take  established scales and questionnaires  and measures for those things take that  questionnaire that's got say 10 15 20  statements that you say very untrue or  very true of you about gambling and you  just replace the word gambling with  gaming so you've got this new gaming  addiction questionnaire now and you give  that to a thousand people and you will  get a spread of scores right some people  who score zero some people score 100 and  you decide if you score 60 or more  you're considered addicted to games are  you measuring gaming addiction though  because the way that those statements  were originally constructed makes sense  for gambling maybe doesn't make so much  sense for gaming we've never really got  past that problem one major issue with  all of this is that there's very little  concrete evidence showing how much of  the population might be affected in its  reasoning the who points to a number of  reviews it commissioned studying the  prevalence of gaming disorder across the  world but the results across these  studies suggest the condition might  affect anywhere from  0.21 to 57.5  of the gaming population the results are  so varied that even the authors of the  who commissioned report urged a cautious  approach but this is a really important  thing to get right in 2019 a survey of  over 2 000 parents found that half of  them feared their children were becoming  addicted to playing games but with no  concrete evidence to show exactly how  many gamers are affected it's hard to  know how well-founded those fears might  be the range of 0.1.2  up to  more than 50  is a huge range right so something's  wrong there and we're kind of rushing to  do research in this area because  everybody's worried about fortnight  being addictive or whatever because  that's what we see in the news and  parents care about this sort of stuff  and it's understandable why but we're  not taking the time to just slow down  and  think about how we study this  objectively and  however outside of research psychology  the who's decision had another  potentially more significant impact on  those people who have actually been  diagnosed with the condition as a direct  result of the addition of gaming  disorder to the icd-11 specialist  centers began to open offering treatment  for the condition in london we visited  the national center for gaming disorders  the only state-funded uk clinic  dedicated to treating the condition and  spoke to rebecca lockwood a consultant  clinical psychologist about the impact  of the condition on those who suffer  from it for the people that we see in  the clinic we've been struck by actually  quite quite how much of an impact this  has on the game of themselves and the  families often there's kind of violence  and aggression often they are involved  in other services mental health services  social services are involved we've been  kind of struck by the amount of kind of  risk for those that  struggle with this addiction also the  impact on physical health malnourishment  often people are quite underweight  because they're not feeding themselves  on average we we would see people gaming  maybe 10-12 hours a day and that would  be often during night that will lead to  difficulties with attending school and  work so we're not we're not talking  about a few hours we're talking about  considerable period of that person's  life that  is spent gaming since opening in 2019 a  year after the who's decision the clinic  reports it has been overwhelmed with  referrals for treatment 70 of which are  for people aged 18 or under in many  cases gaming begins as an immersive and  rewarding way to escape from reality  something that i'd imagine resonates  with many of us who play games but that  escape becomes an unhealthy compulsion  we have seen within those that have  self-selected to come to the clinic  quite high levels of autistic spectrum  disorder and adhd and also high levels  of social anxiety we've seen people that  have come to the clinic because there's  been an event in their life such as  bullying or parents divorce or kind of  transition from junior to senior school  and often that's been the kind of  trigger for them to start to use gaming  as an escape and then the gaming in  itself has become kind of a secondary  problem there's some kind of patterns  even though we can't kind of quantify  them at the moment but we have seen some  patterns of people that typically refer  to the clinic many of the arguments  against including gaming disorder in the  icd-11 center around the lack of  evidence that gaming itself is causing  harm but does that actually matter if  the result is a clinic that helps people  deal with both the symptom and the  underlying cause following treatment  many patients at the national center for  gaming disorders report an improvement  in their quality of life and a healthier  relationship to gaming it's important to  know that unlike some press outlets the  clinic does not demonize gaming at all  through their treatment nor do they  support banning gaming entirely  something that came as a surprise  instead they're well aware of the  positive impact gaming can have we're  looking at not abstaining which might be  how you treat some other  addictions but actually changing the  balance and the relationship because  often it will be that gaming has  completely taken over that person's life  so we start to look at how to  reintroduce other activities to start to  motivate people to go back to school or  work addressing some of the underlying  factors and enabling them to function in  a way where they don't need to use  gaming as a  kind of coping strategy  it's not a surprise that clinical  psychologists like rebecca are in favor  of the who's decision when they work  directly with patients clearly for some  people gaming can have a really negative  influence on their relationships and  their mental health the decision to  define gaming disorder has in that sense  done some good as it's enabled people to  access the care and support that they  need for some though the concern is that  with little evidence to pinpoint how  much of the population are actually  affected some people may slip through  the cracks my worry with it all is that  we're not capturing the people we need  to capture there are treatments that  will do something right but you don't  want to be treating a symptom you want  to be treating the cause so you don't  want people to relapse or relapse into  another form of addiction i don't think  the research base is good enough i think  we need to do a lot of work before we  get to that point one thing that clearly  doesn't help is the sort of  sensationless reporting we talked about  earlier dramatic headlines risk  demonizing gaming among non-gamers as  something that's incredibly toxic and  harmful but it also risks creating a  sense among gamers that this is simply a  moral panic and that psychologists are  just trying to take their games away  from them also fortnight as it turns out  is not quite the problem some would have  you believe gaming generally is healthy  so like we talked about about the  balance and the impact and the  associated distress so for most people  this will not be  a problem i think the media can help  increase understanding and awareness but  i think it has a responsibility to be  kind of proportional in its discussions  and actually the majority of our young  people probably don't pay for like  there's a range of different games but  often that's not one of the  ones that they play rebecca wouldn't be  drawn on which games they do see a lot  but some clinical psychologists have  called for governmental bodies to  regulate the games industry and hold  them accountable for the impact of games  with gameplay loops that could encourage  compulsive play unsurprisingly so far  games developers have been unwilling to  share data with addiction specialists  that could shed some light on how people  engage with their games given the lack  of progress regulating something like  loot boxes any meaningful regulation on  gaming addiction seems unlikely  speaking of which next week's episode of  mind games is all about loot boxes so  make sure you're subscribed and check  your feeds for that next week in the  short term at least i find it kind of  comfort to know that people are  researching games and the effect that  they can have on us in our mental health  and also that clinics are available for  people who may feel like they have a  problem if you're watching this video  and you feel like you might need to  speak to somebody about addiction there  are links below the video to services in  both the uk and the us that can help  i'll see you next time
 

